(* Fork-join parallelism, and nothing else.

   Both functions take the most workers to run at once as their first
   argument, stated rather than inferred: how much a script may do at the
   same time is a decision about the machine it runs on.

   Workers never outlive the call. There is no handle to a running one, no
   way to start one outside these two functions, and nothing to await -- so
   there is no unstructured concurrency to build out of them, and no
   function needs a different colour for being called from one.

   A worker is never outside a handler's reach. With nothing watching it
   performs its own effects on its own domain, so twenty slow commands
   really do overlap. With a handler in scope -- a mock, `--dry-run`,
   `--trace` -- effects are carried back to the calling domain and taken one
   at a time, because an effect cannot reach a handler on another domain.
   So a test can never be bypassed by moving work into `Par`: being watched
   costs the overlap, and nothing rehearses for speed. *)

(** Apply `f` to every element, at most `limit` at a time, and collect the
    results in the list's own order. An element whose work raises comes back
    as an `Error` in its place rather than failing the others. *)
let map limit f xs = par_map limit f xs

(** Apply `f` to every element for its effects, at most `limit` at a time.
    A failure raises once the work is done. *)
let each limit f xs = par_each limit f xs

(** Run every thunk at once and answer with the first to finish. The rest
    are told to stop, and every one of them is joined before this returns.
    
    First to *finish*, not first to succeed: a winner that raised comes back
    as `Error`, and a loser that raised is discarded.
    
    There is no worker limit. The count is the length of the list, and it is
    already at the call site.
    
    What a race bounds is when you get the answer, not when the machine goes
    quiet. A loser blocked in a subprocess finishes that subprocess; wrap it
    in `Shell.timeout` to bound that too. *)
let race thunks = par_race thunks
